  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nDry and mild conditions persist across the Northern Rockies and MT \nthrough much of the upcoming work week as large scale ridging \nremains centered across the SW US with the ridge flattening some \nafter Monday. The warmest daytime temperatures are on track for \nMonday as increased mid-level westerly flow over the flattening \nridge provides some additional warming to the lee of the Rockies. \nThis also results in increased surface winds on Monday, particularly \nacross north-central MT where gusts in excess of 30 mph are likely \nin areas adjacent to the Rockies and central MT mountains. After \npeaking around 20 degrees above seasonal averages Monday with a few \nlocations potentially approaching daily records, temperatures are \nlikely to remain 10-15 degrees above average through Friday. Hoenisch\n\nConfidence is increasing for a significant change in the current \nweather pattern beginning Saturday due to the development of an \nupper level trough and cold air moving into the region. While \nSaturday is expected to be 10-20 degrees cooler than Friday, there \nis increasing confidence that this will be followed by another 5-15 \ndegree drop between Saturday and Sunday. Additionally, while there \nis uncertainty with accumulation amounts, most models are beginning \nto agree that there is the potential for some form of widespread \nsnowfall Saturday night into the beginning of the following week. \nActual accumulations will likely be dependent on the amount of \nmoisture and the timing of the second cool off as the bulk of the \nprecip is currently expected Saturday night through Sunday. ESH\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nThe main weather hazard to watch over the coming days will be \nelevated fire weather concerns developing Monday as anomalously warm \nafternoon temperatures combine with low afternoon humidity and \nbreezy to locally windy conditions. This is most pronounced across \nareas areas from the east slopes of the Rocky Mtn Front eastward \nacross areas adjacent to the central MT mountains where wind gusts \nin excess of 30-35 mph are most likely. While fuels are not \ncurrently critical, these conditions will begin a trend toward \nhigher fire danger in the upcoming week. Hoenisch\n\nWhile there is still uncertainty with the potential widespread snow \nevent next weekend, confidence is increasing for snow accumulations \nof over 1\" for western and central Montana. Models are currently \nfavoring the Rockies and central Montana for the highest totals, \nalthough most models do have total snow accumulations remaining \nunder 5\". It is worth noting, as we are still many days out from the \npotential event, we could still see large swings in the model \noutputs, especially for total snow accumulations. ESH",
